            "title": "The War on Trade: Farmers, ag business bear brunt of hit",
            "snippets": [
              "# The War on Trade: Farmers, ag business bear brunt of hit\n“We have lost track of how many times Washington has indicated the trade war is almost over,” said Mark Albertson, director of strategic market development for the Illinois Soybean Association. “During the past 12 months, there have been some ups and downs, but mostly downs, unfortunately. Most of the soy that is grown in Illinois gets exported, about 60 percent, and half of that would typically go to China. When China retaliated against U.S. tariffs by putting on their own tariffs on U.S",
              "But then things got “crazy and bizarre,” added Albertson. China stopped purchasing from the United States but Argentina — which exported a record amount of soy to China — actually had to start buying from American farmers.",
              "“We are deeply concerned about the imposition of the retaliatory tariffs that could further impact agriculture,” he said. “Tariffs historically have not been good for the farm economy. Farmers are on the front lines of this trade war and are sacrificing their livelihoods and it hasn’t gotten better. We are sitting on a huge inventory of grain while our export markets are diminishing. … Corn and soybean prices are depressed, combined with a delayed 2019 planting season due to heavy rains, and farmers are facing their sixth straight year of declining net farm income.”",
              "The future certainly isn’t bright for the state’s soybean farmers. The U.S. Department of Agricultur e estimates soybean exports at 1.7 billion bushels, down 434 million bushels from 2017-18",
              "“The current state of Chinese tariffs on U.S. soybeans and the continuing spread of African swine fever does not bode well for U.S. soybean exports,” University of Illinois agricultural economist Todd Hubbs said. \"Chinese soybean purchases remain contingent on progress in negotiations. Based on previous negotiation outcomes, a decent probability exists that tariffs stay in place through the next marketing year.\"",
              "The roundtable discussion with Sen. Dick Durbin consisted of topics ranging from climate change to tariffs on China. With the looming fear of an early frost with a late plant, local agriculture leaders work to make efforts to lessen the potential blow.\n\nJEFF SMUDDE, HERALD & REVIEW\n\nIllinois exported $1.3 billion in soybeans and $8 million in corn to China in 2017. That fell to $94 million in soybeans and $5 million in corn in 2018",
