              "Europe is a market in which U.S. soybean exports have fared well, with the market share of the American oilseeds expected to hit 75% of imports this year — up from 30% previously, said Sutter",
              "{\"headline\":\"US soybean farmers are working new markets now that exports to China have tanked\",\"dateCreated\":\"2019-08-08T02:58:53+0000\",\"datePublished\":\"2019-08-08T02:58:53+0000\",\"dateModified\":\"2019-08-08T16:00:14+0000\",\"author\":[{\"name\":\"Huileng Tan\"}],\"publisher\":{\"name\":\"CNBC\",\"foundingDate\":\"1989-04-17\",\"sameAs\":[\"https://www.facebook.com/cnbc\",\"https://www.instagram.com/cnbc\",\"https://www.linkedin.com/company/cnbc\",\"https://twitter.com/cnbc\",\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CNBC\",\"https://www.youtube.com/cnbc\"]},\"articleSection\":\"Markets\",\"keywords\":[\"cnbc\",\"Articles\",\"Pakistan\",\"Egypt\",\"Southeast Asia\",\"Soybeans (Nov'25)\",\"United States\",\"Agriculture\",\"World economy\",\"Economy\",\"Trade\",\"Politics\",\"Business\",\"China\",\"Asia News\",\"Markets\",\"Asia Economy\",\"Business News\",\"World Economy\",\"Asia Markets\",\"Commodities: Alternative Investing\",\"Futures & Commodities\",\"source:tagname:CNBC Asia Source\"],\"video\":{\"description\":\"Farmers are losing their fourth-largest customer as China officially stops buying U.S. agricultural products. The loss piles onto an already tough year for crops and commodity prices and could dent U.S. gross domestic product and sales at companies whose businesses are tied to farming in the Heartland. These three numbers showcase how American farmers have already felt the impact of the escalating trade war.\",\"duration\":\"PT2M42S\",\"name\":\"Three numbers show how US farmers have already been hit by the trade war\",\"uploadDate\":\"2019-08-06T20:04:46+0000\"}}"
            ]
